You should not take carvedilol if you are allergic to it, or if you have:
asthma, bronchitis, emphysema;
severe liver disease; or
A serious heart condition such as heart block, "sick sinus syndrome," or slow heart rate exists unless you have a pacemaker.
Severe heart failure requiring certain IV medications exists.
To make sure carvedilol is safe for you, tell your doctor if you have:
diabetes (taking carvedilol can make it harder for you to tell when you have low blood sugar);
angina (chest pain);
liver or kidney disease;
a thyroid disorder;
pheochromocytoma (tumor of the adrenal gland);
circulation problems (such as Raynaud's syndrome); or
a history of allergies.
FDA pregnancy category C. It is not known whether carvedilol will harm an unborn baby.
Tell your doctor if you are pregnant or plan to become pregnant while using this medication.
It is not known whether carvedilol passes into breast milk.
It is not known if it could harm a nursing baby.
Talk with your doctor about the best way to feed your baby if you are taking carvedilol.

